-- | The widest pipeline written on one line.
-- |
-- | This is rustfmt's `chain_width`, which `redox-emit` already pins as
-- | `CHAIN_WIDTH` for the same subject: `|>` lowers one stage to one method
-- | segment, so a pipeline too wide for one line of generated Rust is too wide
-- | for one line of the Redox it came from. Taking the emitter's number keeps
-- | both sides of the compiler breaking the same chain in the same place.
-- |
-- | It is a second constant rather than a shared one because sharing it would
-- | run the dependency the wrong way: `redox-fmt` prints Redox and has no
-- | business importing the emitter to find out how wide a line is.
-- |
-- | # Measured on the line, where the emitter measures the segments
-- |
-- | `CHAIN_WIDTH`'s own doc explains why it leaves the receiver out — rustfmt
-- | does. Here the receiver is counted, along with the indentation, because
-- | this layout puts the receiver on the first line and hangs the stages
-- | beneath it, so the receiver is part of the line being measured.
-- |
-- | What still cannot be counted is the text to the *left* of the expression:
-- | `expr_text` does not know whether it is printing after `evens_doubled xs = `
-- | or after `print (`. That is the honest limit of the measurement, and it errs
-- | towards leaving a line long rather than breaking one that fits.
export(crate) const PIPELINE_WIDTH : usize = 60
